children.text:
A domain's specification opens with its header. The header is a node stating:

- what the domain does;
- its owners, where it names them (§s-6sk8uet75dmq5qz);
- its level (optional, used when the domains are visualized).

The header's `consumes` links name the sections of other domains the domain uses. Domains do not form a cycle in which they use one another. A notification that one domain sends to another through an event does not make the sender a user of the receiving domain. Such notifications may go in either direction. Where the domain has sections, the header also carries their index (§s-2r59txnms5serdr).

children.reason:
Whoever works on one domain loads its header and learns from it alone, without loading the rest:

- what the domain is responsible for;
- who is responsible for its wording;
- how it is divided;
- what it uses from other domains.

A domain cannot work without the domains it uses, so domains that use one another cyclically cannot be built one after another.

children.text:
The index a domain's header carries (§s-yggv9myedprnaa7) is built from the domain's sections and never written by hand. For each section the index lists:

- its sid, its title and its text, which states what the section is responsible for;
- the domains that use it.

The SpecSheriff tools draw a diagram of the domains that shows how the domains are connected (§s-yggv9myedprnaa7). On the diagram:

- domains of the same level are placed in the same row;
- the smaller a level's number, the higher its row;
- domains whose header contains no level are all placed in a single row of their own, below the rows of the levels.
